1
00:00:02,051 --> 00:00:04,889
Sometimes you have code that just fails

2
00:00:05,226 --> 00:00:07,165
under certain circumstances and there's

3
00:00:07,165 --> 00:00:09,169
little you can do about it. Sure, you could

4
00:00:09,169 --> 00:00:11,349
change it such that you don't even try to

5
00:00:11,413 --> 00:00:13,145
write to a file, for example, or that you

6
00:00:13,161 --> 00:00:15,726
always create a file. But let's say we want

7
00:00:15,726 --> 00:00:17,986
to make sure that the user created the file

8
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:20,936
first before we try writing to it. And

9
00:00:21,032 --> 00:00:23,885
therefore here we are perfectly fine with

10
00:00:23,901 --> 00:00:26,722
this line failing occasionally. The thing we

11
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:28,293
should do in such cases,

12
00:00:28,885 --> 00:00:31,513
so if you have code that simply fails under

13
00:00:31,545 --> 00:00:34,013
certain circumstances and you as a developer

14
00:00:34,013 --> 00:00:36,865
can't really prevent it, in such cases you

15
00:00:36,961 --> 00:00:39,108
wanna use try-catch.

16
00:00:40,006 --> 00:00:42,265
Try-catch is a built-in mechanism in

17
00:00:42,265 --> 00:00:44,716
JavaScript and therefore also available in

18
00:00:44,748 --> 00:00:46,335
Deno which allows you

19
00:00:46,928 --> 00:00:50,981
to try a certain code but catch a potential

20
00:00:51,142 --> 00:00:54,346
error without crashing your script. So if

21
00:00:54,346 --> 00:00:55,292
this code fails,

22
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:58,787
the script will now not fail if you wrapped

23
00:00:58,787 --> 00:00:59,428
it with try.

24
00:01:00,053 --> 00:01:02,169
Instead, you will end up in this catch block

25
00:01:02,602 --> 00:01:05,376
if this line or whatever is inside of try

26
00:01:05,728 --> 00:01:06,113
fails.

27
00:01:06,931 --> 00:01:09,976
And now here you can have some fallback

28
00:01:10,073 --> 00:01:11,820
logic. For example, here we could let the

29
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:12,285
user know

30
00:01:13,102 --> 00:01:18,136
file user_input.txt does not exist, please

31
00:01:19,050 --> 00:01:19,755
create it.

32
00:01:21,657 --> 00:01:24,666
First. Now this is a perfectly fine fallback

33
00:01:24,973 --> 00:01:27,788
where we inform the user what went wrong and

34
00:01:27,885 --> 00:01:30,053
what the user of our script should do to

35
00:01:30,069 --> 00:01:30,942
make it work again.

36
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:33,796
And the next time when the user did create

37
00:01:33,796 --> 00:01:36,344
that file, the next time the script runs, it

38
00:01:36,441 --> 00:01:36,844
will work.

39
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:40,883
So try-catch is a very useful tool for

40
00:01:40,932 --> 00:01:43,948
situations where something might just fail.

41
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:47,425
As a side note, when interacting with files

42
00:01:47,898 --> 00:01:50,339
it's always a great idea to use try-catch

43
00:01:50,756 --> 00:01:53,165
even if you don't have that createFalse

44
00:01:53,326 --> 00:01:53,663
option.

45
00:01:54,369 --> 00:01:54,498
Why?

46
00:01:55,108 --> 00:01:57,710
Because working with files can easily fail

47
00:01:57,854 --> 00:01:59,765
because of missing permissions on the user

48
00:01:59,765 --> 00:02:03,009
side, because of some third-party software

49
00:02:03,138 --> 00:02:05,691
like an antivirus tool which simply blocks

50
00:02:05,691 --> 00:02:08,308
access. So there are a lot of reasons why

51
00:02:08,678 --> 00:02:11,472
writing to a file could fail even if it

52
00:02:11,488 --> 00:02:12,484
theoretically shouldn't.

53
00:02:13,078 --> 00:02:16,047
And it's beyond your control. You being the

54
00:02:16,047 --> 00:02:18,355
developer. It's beyond your control. You

55
00:02:18,371 --> 00:02:20,551
can't know in advance when you write this

56
00:02:20,551 --> 00:02:23,099
code whether it might eventually fail. And

57
00:02:23,115 --> 00:02:25,567
therefore, try-catch is great for such

58
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:26,288
situations.

59
00:02:27,121 --> 00:02:29,061
Of course, you should not put your entire

60
00:02:29,189 --> 00:02:31,946
program into the try block. There are a lot

61
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:34,734
of errors which you can rule out in advance

62
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:35,552
as a developer.

63
00:02:36,096 --> 00:02:38,052
But there are certain situations like this

64
00:02:38,084 --> 00:02:41,353
one where you can't be 100% certain that it

65
00:02:41,369 --> 00:02:43,789
will work and there's nothing you can do to

66
00:02:43,789 --> 00:02:46,658
force it to work well then try-catch is a

67
00:02:46,658 --> 00:02:47,507
great fallback.

68
00:02:48,437 --> 00:02:50,135
So here in this case, if I now run this

69
00:02:50,183 --> 00:02:52,635
again, you see now it does not crash.

70
00:02:53,004 --> 00:02:54,750
Instead, it gives us this useful

71
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:55,423
information.

72
00:02:56,048 --> 00:02:58,548
And thereafter, you see this code ran just

73
00:02:58,612 --> 00:03:01,481
fine because it didn't crash. It just didn't

74
00:03:01,577 --> 00:03:04,109
execute this single line here successfully.

75
00:03:04,942 --> 00:03:06,897
And now we know what we can do. We can

76
00:03:06,945 --> 00:03:09,221
create a user_input.txt file

77
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:12,155
which is empty here And if we now run that

78
00:03:12,155 --> 00:03:12,508
again,

79
00:03:13,262 --> 00:03:14,161
now you see

80
00:03:14,803 --> 00:03:15,813
we're not getting an error.

81
00:03:16,712 --> 00:03:18,718
But there also is something else you might

82
00:03:18,718 --> 00:03:19,006
see,

83
00:03:19,921 --> 00:03:22,906
and that's the fact that our script never

84
00:03:22,906 --> 00:03:26,163
pauses to allow the user to enter something.

85
00:03:26,676 --> 00:03:28,762
Instead, it just runs from start to end in

86
00:03:28,923 --> 00:03:29,436
one go,

87
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:32,501
and we're not even able to enter something

88
00:03:32,838 --> 00:03:33,046
here.

89
00:03:33,736 --> 00:03:35,662
So we're not able to enter what should be

90
00:03:35,662 --> 00:03:36,496
stored in the file.

91
00:03:37,411 --> 00:03:39,769
Well, let's see why this happens next.
